# Anchored to the repository root on purpose. An unanchored "world/"
# matches any directory of that name at any depth, and git turns on
# core.ignorecase on Windows, so it quietly swallowed src/World/ -- the
# whole terrain module -- and the repository would not build from a
# clean clone. Keep the leading slash on anything with a generic name.
/build/
/build-web/
/out/
/world/
/.cache/
/browsercraft-netlify.zip
compile_commands.json
*.log
*.user

# Resource packs and audio are other people's work. They live on your
# machine, never in the repository and never in the published build:
#   assets/textures/  Prime's HD Textures (supporter download, all rights reserved)
#   assets/sounds/    Minecraft 1.12 audio (Mojang)
#   assets/skins/     whatever you drop in
# The engine paints its own fallbacks for all three, which is what
# browsercraft.net actually ships.
/assets/textures/
/assets/sounds/
/assets/skins/
